# The inventory of every variable a production deploy sets. Copy to `.env.prod` ON THE SERVER,
# fill it in, and keep it out of git — `.gitignore` already refuses it.
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod && chmod 600 .env.prod
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod up -d --build
#
# Nothing generates this file and nothing generates the one you write from it — it is the whole
# description of the surface, and `src/scripts/prod-env.test.ts` is what keeps it honest against
# `src/backend/config.ts` and the compose file.
#
# THIS FILE FEEDS COMPOSE'S INTERPOLATION, NOT A CONTAINER. `--env-file` substitutes the `${VAR}`
# expressions inside `docker-compose.prod.yml`; it never becomes the environment of a process. A
# name added here and not named there is deployed, correct, and read by nobody — which is the whole
# reason that test exists.
#
# TWO SURFACES. `EAIT__DEPLOY__*` belongs to the box — the edge and the database, whose images have
# their own variable names that the compose file maps ours onto. `EAIT__BACKEND__*` is read by
# `src/backend/config.ts`, which is the inventory this half is checked against. Every default below
# is the one `configDefaults()` carries, so deleting a line is safe and changing one is a decision.
#
# NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY:
#   EAIT__BACKEND__DATABASE_URL   composed by docker-compose.prod.yml from the password below, so
#                                 the credential exists in exactly one place.
#   EAIT__BACKEND__HOST/PORT      fixed by the image: 0.0.0.0:8787 inside a container that publishes
#                                 no port. An operator has nothing to decide and nothing to get
#                                 wrong.
#   EAIT__FRONTEND__*             the web application's own. HOST and PORT are set on its service;
#                                 EAIT__FRONTEND__BACKEND_ORIGIN is the LAPTOP's dev proxy and must
#                                 stay unset here — in production the edge composes the one origin.

# ── The box ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ONE hostname this deployment serves. Its A record must already point at this server before the
# first `up`: Caddy proves control over port 80 to get a certificate, and Let's Encrypt rate-limits
# failed validations to five per hostname per hour.
#
# One name, not two. The browser client calls its API with relative paths under `connect-src 'self'`
# and the backend carries no CORS header anywhere — `src/frontend/AGENTS.md` is where that is
# written down. Splitting this across two hostnames breaks the product's security model, not just
# its routing.
EAIT__DEPLOY__DOMAIN=eait.example.com

# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. An address somebody reads.
EAIT__DEPLOY__ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Generate, do not invent:  openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -d '\n=+/'
#
# Only ever consumed by initdb, on an EMPTY volume. Changing it later does not change the database's
# password — it leaves the backend unable to log in to its own Postgres. Rotate with ALTER ROLE
# inside psql first, then update this.
EAIT__DEPLOY__POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# How long the edge holds a request open for the backend. A BACKSTOP, never the shortest hop: one
# turn can spend more than one model call, and a 504 manufactured here arrives while the backend is
# still running the call and still being billed for it. Keep it above EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_TIMEOUT_MS
# by a wide margin.
EAIT__DEPLOY__PROXY_TIMEOUT=600s

# ── The model ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED. Every photo is a billed vision call on this key. The server refuses to start without it.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_API_KEY=
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter
# The analyzer and the router. Needs vision.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.5
# The coach — the model behind a question in chat. Text only, so it need not be the vision model,
# and its own setting so the two can move independently.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_CHAT_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.6
# One sentence about the plate while the analyzer works, on a model that does NOT reason. Empty
# disables the glance and the pending screen keeps its scripted line.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_GLANCE_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.3
# low | medium | high, sent as reasoning.effort on every schema call. Empty = the model decides.
# A value the provider rejects is a 400 on every call, and a 400 is charged.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=
# Point this at a proxy or a gateway without touching code.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions
# How long ONE model call may hang, and the number the client sizes its own wait from. Floored at
# 10s: zero is a budget of nothing, not "no limit".
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=90000
# The completion bound sent with every call. Omitting it does not mean "no limit" — the provider
# substitutes the model's own ceiling and reserves the whole of it against the balance before
# routing, so an unbounded request is refused as a 402 on a balance that would have covered the real
# call many times over. A measured meal analysis is ~1600 completion tokens. Floored at 2000.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_MAX_TOKENS=16000

# ── Spend ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A public URL with an uncapped model key is a bill, not a product. There is no free tier: an account
# gets FREE_ANALYSES analyses over its LIFETIME and every later one is refused with 402 until the
# purchase webhook below has written an entitlement. With the webhook unset nobody is ever entitled,
# so an instance with no store behind it is a sample of itself — which is a fine thing to self-host.
#
# GLOBAL_DAILY_ANALYSIS_CAP sits above both: a subscription buys a larger share of the instance
# budget, never an exemption from it. 0 disables a cap entirely and is the wrong answer on a public
# instance.
EAIT__BACKEND__FREE_ANALYSES=15
EAIT__BACKEND__PAID_DAILY_PHOTO_CAP=200
EAIT__BACKEND__GLOBAL_DAILY_ANALYSIS_CAP=500

# Per-address limits, which are what make the per-account caps mean anything: minting an account is
# one unauthenticated request. In memory, so a restart forgives everyone. Generous on purpose — a
# mobile carrier puts thousands of subscribers behind one address.
EAIT__BACKEND__AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=20
EAIT__BACKEND__ANALYSIS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_DAY=60
EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=5
EAIT__BACKEND__HEALTH_SYNC_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=120
EAIT__BACKEND__LINES_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=120

# ── Uploads, sessions, dates ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE, and sent to the client in the profile response so it offers exactly as many
# photo slots as this instance will accept. Never also hardcoded in a client: two numbers that must
# agree are two numbers that eventually will not. Keep the edge's body limit above
# MAX_UPLOAD_MB × MAX_PHOTOS_PER_MEAL plus multipart overhead.
EAIT__BACKEND__MAX_UPLOAD_MB=20
EAIT__BACKEND__MAX_PHOTOS_PER_MEAL=4
# How long a meal proposed in chat stays confirmable.
EAIT__BACKEND__PENDING_TTL_MINUTES=30
# Days a bearer token survives UNUSED. The stored form is a hash, so a database dump names accounts
# and login times and hands nobody a way to act as one. At least 1.
EAIT__BACKEND__SESSION_TTL_DAYS=180
# IANA zone for every date boundary. Not UTC: a daily total whose midnight is UTC rolls over at
# 01:00 local, so a late dinner lands on tomorrow.
EAIT__BACKEND__TZ_NAME=Europe/Berlin

# ── The two public URLs this server builds links from ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Stated rather than read off a request header, which a client can influence — and one of these ends
# up in an email a person is expected to trust.
#
# ON THIS DEPLOYMENT THEY ARE THE SAME VALUE, and that is the point of one origin: the API and the
# browser front door are one hostname, so a sign-in's redirect_uri, the `/start` front door and a
# confirmation link all name it. Both must be `https://` + EAIT__DEPLOY__DOMAIN.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_API_URL=https://eait.example.com
EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_WEB_URL=https://eait.example.com

# ── Signing in ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CLIENT IDS ARE NOT SECRETS — they identify an app, they do not authorise anything. They are here
# because the backend must know which audiences to ACCEPT: a token minted for a different app is a
# valid token and an invalid login, and only the audience check catches that. An EMPTY list switches
# that provider off and its route refuses rather than verifying blind.
#
# A SELF-HOSTED INSTANCE CAN LEAVE ALL OF THIS EMPTY. `/start` then answers 404 and the only way in
# is the Telegram connector below, or a client that mints a device account.
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_AUDIENCES=
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_AUDIENCES=

# Onboarding in a browser, with Google. BOTH OR NEITHER: with either empty every path under /start
# answers 404. The id must ALSO be listed in EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_AUDIENCES above — the server
# REFUSES TO START otherwise, because the token this flow gets back carries the id as its audience
# and every callback would fail verification after the person had already chosen their account.
# Register as the authorized redirect URI:
#   ${EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_WEB_URL}/start/auth/google/callback
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Sign in with Apple, in a browser. ALL FOUR OR NONE — with any of them empty the Apple button is
# not offered and its routes 404; Google alone still works, and vice versa.
#
# IT IS A DIFFERENT CLIENT FROM AN APP'S. A browser authorises as a SERVICE ID, so the SERVICE ID
# must ALSO be listed in EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_AUDIENCES above — beside any bundle id, never instead
# of it. Apple issues no client secret: it issues a KEY, and the secret is a five-minute ES256 JWT
# this server mints from it per request. Write the .p8 on ONE LINE with \n for the newlines, BEGIN
# and END lines included; anything that is not a PKCS#8 PEM is a startup error rather than a
# callback that fails later. Return URL to register:
#   ${EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_WEB_URL}/start/auth/apple/callback
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_SERVICE_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_TEAM_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_KEY_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ── The Telegram connector ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SHIPS DORMANT, and this empty value is what keeps it that way. Set it and the same process runs a
# bot: get a token from @BotFather, and use a separate bot for development.
#
# IT NEEDS NO INBOUND ROUTE. The connector polls api.telegram.org over outbound HTTPS, so there is
# no proxy block for it, no port to open and nothing to publish. The only firewall requirement is
# that outbound 443 works, which the default-allow-outbound policy in `src/iac/` already gives it.
EAIT__BACKEND__TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# ── The admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The admin is a ROLE an account carries, not a shared secret. This is the one account granted it at
# boot — a UUID from `users.id`, re-applied on every start so a restored dump cannot leave an
# instance nobody can administer. EMPTY means nobody holds the role and every path under /admin
# answers 404, which is the correct default. Sign in once, read the id off the account, then set it.
EAIT__BACKEND__ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_USER_ID=

# ── The paid tier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The store takes the payment, RevenueCat is told, and RevenueCat posts here. There is no other path
# by which an account becomes paid. EMPTY means /v1/revenuecat/webhook answers 404 and nobody is
# ever entitled — correct for an instance that is not selling anything, which is most of them.
#
# Compared against the WHOLE Authorization header, byte for byte. Under 24 characters or containing
# whitespace is a startup error, not a webhook anybody can post to.
EAIT__BACKEND__REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
# Which entitlement grants the tier. Must match the identifier in the RevenueCat dashboard: one that
# exists nowhere means every delivery is accepted and nothing is granted, while every customer is
# charged, and the only trace is a warning line per purchase.
EAIT__BACKEND__REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_ID=eait_fit_pro
# 1 accepts SANDBOX / Test Store events. Only while purchases are being exercised from a test build:
# a simulated purchase must not grant a real tier on the day a listing goes live.
EAIT__BACKEND__REVENUECAT_ACCEPT_SANDBOX=0
# Where the plan page sends somebody to subscribe. MUST contain {userId} when set at all — a
# checkout link with no account id in it produces a purchase this server can never apply.
EAIT__BACKEND__WEB_CHECKOUT_URL=

# ── Mail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The one email this product sends. A submission is not a subscription: the address is stored
# pending, one confirmation goes out, and only the link in it adds anybody.
#
# `log` prints the link instead of sending it, which in production means nobody can confirm — the
# server says so at startup. `resend` needs the key below and REFUSES TO START without it, which is
# the correct failure: a provider that silently does not send leaves a form that thanks people and a
# list that never grows.
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_PROVIDER=log
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_FROM=eait <lets@example.com>
EAIT__BACKEND__RESEND_API_KEY=
EAIT__BACKEND__RESEND_BASE_URL=https://api.resend.com
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# How long an unconfirmed row survives before it is deleted. An address somebody typed and never
# confirmed is personal data with no basis for holding it — quite possibly somebody else's address,
# typed by a stranger. At least 1.
EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=7
# How many addresses the form may add in a rolling day, across everyone.
EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_DAILY_CAP=200
# Where to send a browser after it posts the subscribe form. EMPTY means the routes answer JSON
# instead of redirecting, which is what an instance with no marketing site wants — this repository
# has none.
EAIT__BACKEND__LANDING_URL=

# ── Notifications ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OFF unless switched on, and LOGGED rather than sent unless the Expo access token exists: Expo
# accepts unauthenticated sends, so anybody who learns a device token could push to it, and this
# server refuses to make such a send. Switching it on with no token is a deploy that looks configured
# and sends nothing.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUSH_ENABLED=0
EAIT__BACKEND__EXPO_PUSH_ACCESS_TOKEN=
EAIT__BACKEND__PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# HH:MM in EAIT__BACKEND__TZ_NAME, for the evening line. Malformed is a startup error, not a
# fallback — a typo here breaks nothing visible, it just sends at a time nobody chose.
EAIT__BACKEND__EVENING_LINE_TIME=20:30
